window.MANUAL_INDEX = [{"url": "getting-started.html", "title": "Getting started", "summary": "Signing in, finding your way around, notifications and appearance.", "text": "cupcake-master creates and looks after Cupcake instances on a Kubernetes cluster, such as a set of Raspberry Pis. Each instance is a separate Cupcake with its own database, files and address. Signing in Open cupcake-master's address in a browser, for example https://cupcake-k3s.local , and sign in with your email address and password. On a Pi cluster, the first admin login is in cupcake-k3s-credentials.txt on the control plane's boot partition. The sign-in page of cupcake-master. Enter the email address and password of your account, then choose Sign in. A wrong email address or password is refused: A wrong email address or password is refused with a message. After signing in you see your instances: After signing in, cupcake-master opens on the Instances page. Around the screen The top menu leads to every page. Staff see Models, Users, Domains, Backups, Remote access and Logs as well. Icon buttons name their action when the pointer rests on them: Icon buttons name their action when the pointer rests on them. Notifications The bell shows what cupcake-master has to tell you, such as a finished or failed task, an instance over its quota or a certificate about to expire. Unread ones are counted on the bell. The bell opens the latest notifications; unread ones are marked. All of them are on the Notifications page: Notifications lists everything cupcake-master has told you, newest first. Appearance The account menu chooses a light, dark or automatic appearance, and signs you out. Automatic follows your computer. The account menu chooses the light, dark or automatic appearance and signs out. The Instances page lists every Cupcake instance with its state, address, images and owner. In the dark theme, which follows the computer's setting by default."}, {"url": "instances.html", "title": "Instances", "summary": "Creating an instance, its page, its admin account and following its tasks.", "text": "The Instances page lists every Cupcake instance with its state, address, images and owner. Creating an instance Choose New instance on the Instances page. Choose New instance on the Instances page. Enter a slug: 4 to 32 lower-case letters, digits and hyphens. It names the instance's address, for example lab1 becomes https://lab1.local on a Pi cluster. Leave the image tag empty for the current release. Enter a slug of 4 to 32 lower-case letters, digits and hyphens; it names the instance's address. Leave the image tag empty for the current release. Choose Create . The instance's page opens and its provisioning starts. The new instance's page opens; its provisioning is queued. While it runs, the task history shows the step it is on and how long it has been running, and the log below follows it live. While it is provisioned, the task history shows the running task. Once it is active , the instance answers at the address under Domains. Once active, the instance answers at its address, shown under Domains. On a Raspberry Pi the instance is usually active after about 5 minutes. Its ontologies and column templates are then imported from the cluster's reference snapshot as a task of its own, while the instance can already be used. The admin account Provisioning creates a superuser in the new instance for its owner. The username is the part of the owner's email address before the @. The owner sees the password once , at the top of the Admin account section of the instance's page, the first time they open it after provisioning; copy it then. If the password is lost, Reset admin password on the same page sets a new one, again shown once to the owner. Staff can reset it too, but only the owner sees the new password. The instance's page The page shows the instance's state and actions, its admin account, domains, API access, backups, configuration and task history. Staff also see maintenance tasks such as loading reference data. Each instance's own logs are under the Logs button at the top of its page."}, {"url": "api-tokens.html", "title": "API tokens", "summary": "Letting scripts manage an instance through cupcake-master's API.", "text": "A token lets a script manage one instance through cupcake-master's API, as the instance's owner, without staff powers. Tokens are made on the instance's page under API access . API access on an instance's page lists the tokens that let scripts manage it. Creating a token Name the token, choose what it may do and when it expires, then choose Create token . Name the token, choose what it may do and when it expires, then choose Create token. Scope Allows Read Seeing the instance, its tasks and its domains Write Also changing its settings, images, domains, backups and state Admin Also restoring and deleting backups, rotating its secret key and resetting its admin password The token is shown once. Copy it straight away and send it as Authorization: Bearer <token> . Copy the token straight away; it is shown only once. Replacing and revoking Replace the token gives a new one, shown once; the old one stops working at once. Replace the token gives a new one, shown once; the old one stops working at once. Revoke removes it. Revoke removes the token. A token stops working when it expires, is revoked, or its owner's account is suspended. An instance has at most 10 tokens."}, {"url": "domains.html", "title": "Domains and certificates", "summary": "The host names instances and cupcake-master answer on, and their HTTPS certificates.", "text": "Every instance gets an address under the cluster's domain, such as lab1.local . More host names can be added to an instance, or to cupcake-master itself, each reached one of these ways: Reached through Certificate Local network Signed by the cluster's own certificate authority; devices trust it once they trust the cluster Public A free Let's Encrypt certificate; the name must point at the cluster from the internet Your own certificate A certificate and key you upload, for example from your institution HTTPS, self-signed certificate Encrypted, but browsers warn until a signed certificate is uploaded Cloudflare Tunnel Cloudflare's certificate; see remote access Tailscale Tailscale's certificate; the domain becomes a device on your tailnet A host name can only be used once. Under the cluster's domain an instance may use its slug, or its slug followed by -<name> , so instances cannot take each other's names. Plain HTTP requests are redirected to HTTPS. Adding a domain to an instance On the instance's page, Domains lists its host names. Domains on an instance's page lists every host name the instance answers on. Enter the host name and how it is reached, then choose Add domain . Enter another host name, such as ct-b9c044-docs.local, and how it is reached, then choose Add domain. Under the cluster's domain an instance may use its slug followed by -<name>. The instance is updated to answer on it; its programs restart briefly. The domain is added and the instance is updated to answer on it. Remove takes a domain away again. The main domain cannot be removed; make another one the main domain first. Remove takes the domain away again; the main domain cannot be removed. cupcake-master's own domains Domains, in the top menu, lists the host names cupcake-master itself answers on. Domains, in the top menu, lists the host names cupcake-master itself answers on. Add one the same way. Without a certificate for it yet, choose HTTPS, self-signed certificate : Enter the host name, choose HTTPS, self-signed certificate when you have no certificate for it yet, then choose Add domain. The domain is served over HTTPS with a self-signed certificate; browsers warn about it until a signed certificate is uploaded. Using your own certificate Use a signed certificate (or Replace the certificate ) asks for the certificate, with any intermediates, and its key, each as a PEM file. Use a signed certificate asks for the certificate, with any intermediates, and its key. The domain then uses your certificate. Its expiry is shown, and staff are warned two weeks before it. The domain now uses your certificate; its expiry is shown, and staff are warned two weeks before it. Remove takes the domain and its certificate away."}, {"url": "backups.html", "title": "Backups and restore", "summary": "Nightly backups, copying them off the node, how long they are kept, and restoring an instance.", "text": "Every active instance is backed up each night at 02:00: its database and its uploaded files. cupcake-master backs up its own database at 04:30. A backup can also be made at any time with Back up now on an instance's page. Back up now is among the actions at the top of an instance's page. The backup is queued and shown in the task history. When it finishes, the backup is listed under Backups on the instance's page. When it finishes, the backup is listed under Backups, where it can be restored or deleted. How long backups are kept Everything from the last 24 hours is kept, so a backup made before an upgrade stays. Older ones are thinned to the newest backup of each of the 7 most recent days and of the 4 most recent weeks. The same applies to the copies on the backup target. Copying backups off the node Backups are first kept on the cluster's own disk. If that disk fails, they are lost with the data, so set up a backup target on the Backups page in the top menu (staff only). Every night, after cupcake-master's own backup, everything is copied there. Target You need S3-compatible storage The endpoint, bucket, access key and secret key; works with institutional S3, MinIO, Backblaze B2 and Wasabi NFS share The server and export; the export must let the cluster write as root SMB/Windows share The server, share, user, password and domain Choose Test to check the target can be reached and written to, and Copy now to copy straight away. The result of the last copy or test is shown next to the settings. Staff are notified, also by email, when a nightly backup or copy fails. Copies on the target are only removed once they are past the retention above, and never when the cluster has lost its own backups of an instance, so a replaced disk cannot empty the target. Restoring an instance On the instance's page, under Backups, choose Restore this backup next to the backup to go back to, and confirm. This overwrites the instance's database with the backup and puts back the uploaded files it holds; files uploaded since are kept. Delete this backup removes one for good. Rebuilding after losing the cluster With a backup target, everything needed to rebuild is off the node: cupcake-master's database in the _cupcake-master folder and each instance's backups in a folder named after it. Set up a new cluster, restore cupcake-master's database, then restore each instance from its newest backup."}, {"url": "suspend-and-delete.html", "title": "Suspending and deleting instances", "summary": "Stopping an instance for a while, deleting it, and recovering it before it is gone.", "text": "Suspend and resume Suspend stops an instance's programs but keeps its data. Confirm when asked. Suspend stops the instance's programs but keeps its data; confirm when asked. A suspended instance shows Resume instead. A suspended instance shows Resume instead of Suspend. Resume starts it again; it is active once its programs answer. Resume starts it again; it is active once its programs answer. Deleting an instance Only staff can delete an instance. Delete instance is at the bottom of its page and says what is removed and when. Delete instance, at the bottom of an instance's page, says what is removed and when; only staff see it. Type the instance's slug to confirm; the button only works once it matches. Type the instance's slug to confirm; Delete instance is only available once it matches. The instance is stopped at once and kept for 7 days. Its owner is told the date. The instance is stopped at once and kept until the date shown; its owner is told. On the Instances page it is marked with the day it will be deleted. Backups already made are kept. The slug and the instance's domains stay reserved. Recovering a deleted instance Until the date shown, staff can choose Recover , which cancels the deletion and starts the instance again. Its owner cannot resume it while it is waiting to be deleted. Recover, before that date, cancels the deletion and starts the instance again. Deleting at once To delete without waiting, type the slug again under the date and choose Delete now . To delete it without waiting, type its slug again under the date and choose Delete now. Once deleted, its programs, database and files are gone; the page keeps its history. Once deleted, its programs, database and files are gone; the page keeps its history."}, {"url": "remote-access.html", "title": "Remote access", "summary": "Reaching instances and cupcake-master from outside the local network with Cloudflare or Tailscale.", "text": "Remote access, in the top menu (staff only), connects the cluster to a Cloudflare tunnel or to Tailscale. A domain then uses it when it is given the \"Cloudflare Tunnel\" or \"Tailscale\" route on its domains list. Remote access sets up a Cloudflare tunnel or the Tailscale operator for reaching the cluster from outside. Cloudflare Tunnel In Cloudflare Zero Trust, create a tunnel and copy its token. Paste the token here and choose Connect . In the tunnel's Public Hostname list, point each Cloudflare domain, or a wildcard such as *.example.org , at the address shown on this page. Give the domain the \"Cloudflare Tunnel\" route. Tailscale A Tailscale domain becomes its own device on your tailnet, such as lab1.tail1234.ts.net , with a certificate from Tailscale. In the Tailscale admin console, under Settings, OAuth clients, create a client with the Devices (Core) and Auth Keys write scopes and the tag:k8s-operator tag. Your tailnet policy must let tag:k8s-operator own tag:k8s . Enter the client ID and secret here; cupcake-master installs the Tailscale operator. Add the domain, for example lab1.tail1234.ts.net , and give it the \"Tailscale\" route. On a Pi cluster, the Pi itself can also join your tailnet with an auth key in cupcake-tailscale.txt ; see setting up a Pi cluster ."}, {"url": "trust.html", "title": "Trusting the cluster's certificates", "summary": "Making browsers trust the addresses a cluster signs itself, without internet.", "text": "A cluster without internet signs its local addresses, such as https://lab1.local , with its own certificate authority. Browsers warn about those addresses until the device trusts that authority once. The Trust page shows how, for each kind of device, and offers cupcake-ca.crt to download. Its SHA-256 fingerprint is shown so you can check the file. Trust explains how to make devices trust this cluster's own certificate authority. On a Pi cluster the same file is on the control plane's boot partition as cupcake-k3s-ca.crt ."}, {"url": "users.html", "title": "Users and sign-up", "summary": "Approving accounts, instance limits, suspending accounts and the sign-up settings.", "text": "Users, in the top menu (staff only), lists every account with its state and how many instances it may have. Users lists the accounts, their state and instance limit, and the sign-up settings. Sign-up Sign-up can be closed, or open with either email verification or admin approval. With admin approval, new accounts wait until staff Approve or Reject them; staff are notified of each one. Instance limits Each account has a limit on how many instances it may own. Change it on its row. Staff have no limit. Suspending accounts A suspended account cannot sign in, and its API tokens stop working. Staff can suspend ordinary accounts, and superusers can also suspend staff; nobody can suspend an account of their own tier or above. Reactivate lifts it."}, {"url": "models.html", "title": "Transcription models", "summary": "The speech-to-text models kept for every instance.", "text": "Models, in the top menu (staff only), lists the transcription models kept on the cluster for every instance, so they are downloaded once. One of them is the default that new instances use. Models lists the transcription models kept for every instance, and which is the default. Pull downloads a model. Make this the default model changes the default; an instance whose owner chose a model keeps that choice. Download again repairs a model, and Remove from the cache deletes it."}, {"url": "logs.html", "title": "Logs", "summary": "Reading the output of cupcake-master's and each instance's programs.", "text": "Logs, in the top menu (staff only), shows the recent output of cupcake-master's own programs. Each instance's logs are under the Logs button on its page, for its owner and staff. Logs shows the recent output of cupcake-master's own components. Choose the program, how many lines to show, and whether to follow new lines; Download saves them."}, {"url": "pi-cluster.html", "title": "Setting up a Pi cluster", "summary": "Preparing Raspberry Pis as a k3s cluster with files on the boot partition, and checking them.", "text": "A Raspberry Pi becomes a k3s control plane or worker from files copied onto the boot partition of its freshly flashed card, next to the files already there. Each file is read on the next boot. File Does cupcake-k3s.txt Required: ROLE=server for the control plane or ROLE=agent for a worker, and the same TOKEN on every Pi cupcake-wifi.txt Connects to a Wi-Fi network cupcake-storage.txt Keeps k3s's data on a USB drive labelled CUPCAKEDATA instead of the SD card; recommended cupcake-tailscale.txt AUTHKEY joins the Pi to your tailnet; CLIENT_ID and CLIENT_SECRET set up the Tailscale operator cupcake-cloudflared.txt TUNNEL_TOKEN connects a Cloudflare tunnel cupcake-selftest.txt Runs the self-test on that boot Files with secrets are removed from the card once they are used. Problems are written to cupcake-k3s-error.txt , and the admin login to cupcake-k3s-credentials.txt . What a single Pi can do A single Pi is for demonstrations and trying Cupcake out: it runs cupcake-master and one instance. A second instance leaves a 4 GB Pi short of memory, and the first instance on a fresh Pi takes longer to install than later ones. For more instances or real use, add Pis as workers, or run k3s on machines with more memory. Using a USB drive Use an SSD on USB: a USB SSD, or a SATA or NVMe SSD in a USB enclosure. A USB flash stick writes k3s's and the instances' databases so slowly that k3s can stop and restart, so a drive that looks like one is refused; add ALLOW_FLASH=yes to cupcake-storage.txt to use it anyway. The drive must be formatted for Linux: ext4, xfs or btrfs. New drives, and drives formatted on Windows or macOS, are usually exFAT or FAT32. Add FORMAT=yes to cupcake-storage.txt to have the Pi format the drive as ext4 on its first setup; this erases the drive . It only formats a USB drive that is not already ext4, xfs or btrfs. If the drive cannot be used, k3s is not set up and the reason is written to cupcake-k3s-error.txt . Fix it, or remove cupcake-storage.txt to use the SD card, and restart the Pi. Following the setup While a Pi sets up, it shows each step at http://<host name>.local:8080 , for example http://cupcake-k3s.local:8080 , and over Tailscale once it has joined. The page stays for an hour after setup finishes. Each k3s node shows its setup steps at http://<host name>.local:8080 while it sets up. Checking a Pi Copy cupcake-selftest.txt onto its boot partition and restart it. After up to 15 minutes, cupcake-selftest-report.txt next to it says whether setup, k3s, the images, the USB drive, cupcake-master and remote access work. The activity light shows a heartbeat for pass and a fast blink for failure. Keeping the data safe Use a USB SSD with cupcake-storage.txt rather than the SD card, a small UPS, and a backup target so a copy of every night's backups leaves the cluster."}];
